Patch summary:
Give a scanned-out ColorBuffer an explicit DRM format modifier. When the VMM
first exports a color buffer (crosvm does so to hand a scanout to its
display), VkEmulation re-creates the image with VK_EXT_image_drm_format_modifier
from the modifiers the driver can export the format with, in dedicated
dma-buf exportable memory, copies the content over, and reports the chosen
modifier, row pitch and offset through stream_renderer_resource_get_info
(new offset and modifier fields). Any importer (radeonsi, iris on i915 or Xe,
NVIDIA, Asahi) then reads the tiled image correctly, instead of relying on
the layout being implicit in the buffer, which only RADV provides. The
migration is skipped while a guest image or GL texture shares the memory, for
host-visible or multi-planar buffers, and where the driver has no exportable
modifier; those keep the implicit layout (RADV) or stay on readback. The GL
export reports the modifier EGL returns.

